  "notes": "Hook-dense alt-pop with rap-cadenced verses: 95–125 straight, backbeat on 2&4. The harmony is fully documented and unusually flat — G–D–C, I–V–IV, through verse and chorus with a G–D–C–G/B–Am bridge, \"plagal, no dominant resolution; the same three chords run the whole song\" — so `maxChords` is 3, one family dominates and the slash chord in the bridge is read as the same I with the ii appended. The drum signature is the entry's most distinctive fact in the whole pop-rock bench: \"**brushes plus a drum machine** layered together — a programmed 16th pattern under a brushed live kit\", so the velocity tier is soft (a brushed snare has no crack), the hat is pinned to 16ths under it, and the tambourine sits on the off-8ths with `placement` \"offbeat\". \"Effects (wah, tremolo, flange) used on the drum bus as a transition device\" is why the fill schedule is wide — the transitions are not fills. The verse is \"delivered in a hip-hop cadence on 2–3 pitches\" and the chorus is the non-lexical \"doo-doo-doo\" hook, which is `motifPitchCount` 2–5 against a high density and a `call_response` structure — the floor of 2 is the entry's own verse count. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are the velocity tier, every drum probability and the `motifPitchCount` ceiling of 5: the entry counts pitches for the verse only and calls the chorus \"anthemic and stepwise\" without giving it a count; \"brushed for a soft attack\" is a direction and 86/60/24 is this author's reading of it.",
